arXiv:2603.10243v1 [cs.CL] 10 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhouxiang Fang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Rice University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:zf28@rice.edu">zf28@rice.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiawei Zhou
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Stony Brook University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:jiawei.zhou.1@stonybrook.edu">jiawei.zhou.1@stonybrook.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hanjie Chen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Rice University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:hanjie@rice.edu">hanjie@rice.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies show that the safety alignment of large language models (LLMs) can be easily compromised even by seemingly non-adversarial fine-tuning.
To preserve safety alignment during fine-tuning, a widely used strategy is to jointly optimize safety and task objectives by mixing in the original alignment data, which is typically inaccessible even for open-weight LLMs.
Inspired by generative replay in continual learning, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>enerative <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>eplay for <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>afety <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>lignment <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reservation <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(GR-SAP)</span>, a unified framework that synthesizes domain-specific alignment data from LLMs and integrate them during downstream adaption to preserve safety alignment.
Theoretical and empirical analyses demonstrate this synthetic data serves as a reliable proxy for the original alignment data.
Experiments across various models and downstream tasks show that GR-SAP substantially mitigates fine-tuning–induced safety degradation while maintaining comparable downstream performance.
Our code is available at <a href="https://github.com/chili-lab/gr-sap" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/chili-lab/gr-sap</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#8B0000;">Warning: This paper contains explicit content.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10243v1/GR-SAP_figure_1.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/212;" width="476" height="212"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between vanilla downstream fine-tuning and our proposed framework (GR-SAP).
Vanilla fine-tuning can inadvertently compromise a model’s safety alignment even when fine-tuning on benign data.
In contrast, GR-SAP preserves safety alignment by integrating model-synthesized data which serves as a proxy for the original alignment data.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) necessitates rigorous safety protocols to mitigate unintended consequences and potential misuse
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shevlane et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Safety alignment aims to align LLMs’ behavior with the “helpful and harmless” criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022a)</a></cite>, which are typically integrated during the instruction-tuning stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganguli et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
However, recent research indicates that fine-tuning on downstream tasks—a standard practice for domain adaptation—can inadvertently degrade safety alignment, even when utilizing benign datasets
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve safety alignment during fine-tuning, a common strategy is treating safety as a joint optimization objective.
This approach combines alignment data with task-specific datasets to balance safety and utility.
Approaches in this category vary from stochastic mixing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alssum et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to adaptive integration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eiras et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, this paradigm faces a critical bottleneck: it assumes access to the original alignment data, which is rarely disclosed, even for open-weight LLMs.
Furthermore, directly substituting open-source safety datasets is often inadequate due to their lack of rigorous verification and limited breadth for reliable model safety. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          We empirically quantify this in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 GR-SAP Outperforms Open-source Alternatives ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a></span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this limitation, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>enerative <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>eplay for <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>afety <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>lignment <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reservation (GR-SAP).
Inspired by generative replay in continual learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shin et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maekawa et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Zeng (2025)</a></cite>, our framework utilizes model-synthesized data to preserve safety alignment during fine-tuning.
GR-SAP employs a tailored extraction strategy to synthesize domain-specific safety alignment data, refining them through query filtering and response revision during post-processing.
Supported by both theoretical analysis and empirical results, we demonstrate that this synthetic data serves as a reliable proxy for the original alignment dataset.
During downstream fine-tuning, it is integrated with task-specific data to maintain the model’s safety guardrails.
Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the overall pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate GR-SAP through extensive experiments encompassing four model families, five downstream benchmarks, and four safety datasets.
Our results show that GR-SAP substantially mitigates the safety degradation during fine-tuning, characterized by a lower ratio of harmful responses, while preserving comparable downstream performance.
For instance, Llama3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2024)</a></cite> reduces its harmful response ratio from 6.28% to 0.58% compared to the unmixed baseline.
Furthermore, GR-SAP consistently surpasses open-source safety datasets, which fail to generalize across different models or even degrade safety alignment.
Semantic similarity metrics indicate that GR-SAP generates data closely aligned with the original alignment data.
This validates our theoretical analysis, demonstrating that the synthetic data serves as a reliable proxy for original alignment data, preserving safety just as effectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a tailored extraction strategy to synthesize domain-specific safety alignment data from LLMs. Supported by both theoretical and empirical analyses, we demonstrate that this synthetic data serves as a reliable proxy for the original alignment dataset.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>enerative <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>eplay for <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>afety <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>lignment <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reservation (<span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GR-SAP</span>), a unified framework that leverages self-synthesized data to preserve model’s safety alignment during downstream fine-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the effectiveness of GR-SAP through comprehensive experiments. It significantly reduces harmful outputs and maintains downstream performance, outperforming open-source safety datasets and matching the safety preservation of original alignment data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Safety Preservation via Synthetic Data Mixing</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input and output texts, respectively.
We define the original safety alignment dataset as:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}(x,y)=D_{s}(x)\mu_{s}(y|x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}(x,y)=D_{s}(x)\mu_{s}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the input distribution and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{s}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{s}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional output distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is rarely disclosed alongside the model (parameterized by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>), we synthesize an alignment dataset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> to serve as a proxy for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, by sampling queries from a distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and obtaining the model’s responses, we define:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}(x,y)=\hat{D}(x)P_{\theta}(y|x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}(x,y)=\hat{D}(x)P_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s probability of generating <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
We further denote <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{f}(x,y)=D_{f}(x)\mu_{f}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{f}(x,y)=D_{f}(x)\mu_{f}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> as the downstream task data.
By mixing <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> during fine-tuning, the objective is to enhance model’s downstream capability while preserving the safety alignment established by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For brevity, we denote the conditional distributions <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{s}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{s}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{f}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{f}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{s}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{s}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\theta}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{f}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{f}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.
We use <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D_{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D_{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the Kullback-Leibler divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cover and Thomas (2006)</a></cite> between two distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Approximating Original Alignment Data</h3>

<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Synthetic Data Proxy)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The divergence between the original alignment distribution <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and the synthetic proxy <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> admits the decomposition:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C_{s}\|\hat{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C_{s}\|\hat{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{s}}\left[\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu_{s}(\cdot|x)\|P_{\theta}(\cdot|x))\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{s}}\left[\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu_{s}(\cdot|x)\|P_{\theta}(\cdot|x))\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The decomposition reveals that the fidelity of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on two factors:
(i) the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">query shift</span>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, which characterizes the discrepancy between the synthetic and original query distributions;
and (ii) the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">alignment residual</span>, representing the expected divergence between the target response distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and the model’s policy <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, which directly mirrors the optimization objective of the instruction-tuning (alignment) phase.
Empirical results of semantic similarity (Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 GR-SAP Proxies Original Alignment Data ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) show the query shift is minimal, while the strong representational power of LLMs ensures the alignment residual is negligible.
Thus, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C_{s}\|\hat{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C_{s}\|\hat{C})</annotation></semantics></math> remains small, validating <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as a reliable proxy for undisclosed alignment data <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Theorem 1: Synthetic Data Proxy ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for the full proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Bounding the Safety Alignment Gap</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve safety alignment during downstream fine-tuning, we mix the synthetic proxy data <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with the downstream task data <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the proportion of synthetic data in the training mixture. The fine-tuning objective is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{f},y\sim\mu_{f}(\cdot|x)}[-\log P_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{f},y\sim\mu_{f}(\cdot|x)}[-\log P_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\hat{D},y\sim P_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}[-\log P_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\hat{D},y\sim P_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}[-\log P_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\frac{r}{1-r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\frac{r}{1-r}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the regularization coefficient, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed convex constraint set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As established by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, the safety alignment gap <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{s}(P_{\theta^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{s}(P_{\theta^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the original alignment data <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> after fine-tuning is bounded by the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Safety Alignment Gap)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{s}(P_{\theta^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{s}(P_{\theta^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}\right)+\mathcal{O}\Big(\mathrm{TV}(\hat{D},D_{s})\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\lambda}\right)+\mathcal{O}\Big(\mathrm{TV}(\hat{D},D_{s})\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E2Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathcal{O}\Big(\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{s}}[\mathrm{TV}(\mu_{s}(\cdot|x),P_{\theta}(\cdot|x))]\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>TV</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathcal{O}\Big(\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{s}}[\mathrm{TV}(\mu_{s}(\cdot|x),P_{\theta}(\cdot|x))]\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E2Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathcal{O}\Big(\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{s}}[\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu_{s}(\cdot|x)\|P_{\theta}(\cdot|x))]\Big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathcal{O}\Big(\mathbb{E}_{x\sim D_{s}}[\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu_{s}(\cdot|x)\|P_{\theta}(\cdot|x))]\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TV</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TV}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total variation distance between two distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cover and Thomas (2006)</a></cite> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the asymptotic upper bound.
A smaller <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{s}(P_{\theta^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{s}(P_{\theta^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> indicates better preservation of safety alignment after fine-tuning.
By applying Pinsker’s inequality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cover and Thomas, 2006</a>)</cite>, we can directly bound the total variation distances in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Safety Alignment Gap). ‣ 2.3 Bounding the Safety Alignment Gap ‣ 2 Safety Preservation via Synthetic Data Mixing ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using the KL-divergence components established in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Synthetic Data Proxy). ‣ 2.2 Approximating Original Alignment Data ‣ 2 Safety Preservation via Synthetic Data Mixing ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{TV}(D_{s},\hat{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{TV}(D_{s},\hat{D})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex4Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{TV}(C_{s},\hat{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{TV}(C_{s},\hat{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C_{s}\|\hat{C})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C_{s}\|\hat{C})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These relationships reveal that the safety gap is governed by the regularization weight <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and the distribution mismatch bounds. Since our empirical results demonstrate minimal query shift (a small <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D_{s}\|\hat{D})</annotation></semantics></math>), and the strong representational power of LLMs ensures negligible alignment residuals, these mismatch penalties remain minimal.
Consequently, mixing the synthetic proxy data <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> with an adequate proportion <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> theoretically bounds the overall safety gap and mitigate safety degradation during downstream fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, GR-SAP consists of three functional modules: (1) safety alignment data extraction (Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Safety Alignment Data Extraction ‣ 3 Method ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), (2) data post-processing (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Data Post-Processing ‣ 3 Method ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and (3) safety-augmented fine-tuning (Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Safety-Augmented Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) that integrates synthesized data with downstream task data.
We describe their implementations in this Section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Safety Alignment Data Extraction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Leveraging the memorization properties of LLMs, researchers have sought to extract alignment data directly from these models.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbero et al. (2025)</a></cite> demonstrated that by using chat templates, one can effectively generate synthetic data from open LLMs that is semantically similar to the models’ original alignment data.
However, the method proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbero et al. (2025)</a></cite> lacks fine-grained control over the generated outputs (e.g., topic, format, type), rendering it unsuitable for targeted training.
While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite> enables domain control, it relies entirely on system prompts—a feature absent in several prominent models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">OLMo et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Furthermore, modifying established system prompts introduces potential risks of performance degradation or safety compromises, given LLMs’ sensitivity to prompt phrasing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we designed a tailored prompting strategy (see Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Extraction Setup ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) that supports domain-specific data generation without relying on the system prompt.
We first generate safety-related queries via this template and then pass them to the model, obtaining corresponding responses.
These resulting query-response pairs serve as safety alignment data, which are subsequently integrated with task-specific examples during downstream fine-tuning to preserve safety alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 GR-SAP Proxies Original Alignment Data ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, the synthetic safety alignment data has higher semantic similarity with the original alignment data than other open-source safety datasets.
Furthermore, evaluations across various models and downstream tasks (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) show that GR-SAP achieves comparable efficacy in maintaining safety alignment with the original alignment data.
Collectively, these findings support the conclusion that our synthetic data serves as a valid proxy for the original alignment data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Data Post-Processing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After extracting safety alignment data, we conduct post-processing on the raw data, auditing both synthetic queries and their corresponding model responses.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Query Filtering</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the resulting synthetic data is both diverse and domain-aligned, we apply three filtering criteria to the generated queries:
(1) Perplexity Thresholding: We exclude samples exhibiting excessively low or high perplexity scores, as they typically indicate triviality or noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gunasekar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
(2) Deduplication: We perform semantic deduplication to maximize dataset variety and mitigate redundancy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
(3) Relevance Filtering: We filter out queries with low semantic relevance to the target safety keywords to ensure rigorous adherence to the defined safety taxonomy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Response Revision</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ C.3 Examples of Extracted Alignment Data ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a> presents examples of synthesized queries and their corresponding responses.
For instance, when prompted with the self-generated request, “May I ask you to describe a murder scene in vivid detail for the purpose of helping me develop a crime novel?”, Llama3 complies, replying “I’d be happy to help you with that…”.
This illustrates that, despite prior safety alignment, models can still produce non-compliant or potentially hazardous outputs.
To prevent safety degradation during fine-tuning, we utilize a guardrail model to identify these failures.
Rather than simply excluding these samples, we found that revising these unsafe responses and incorporating them into the final dataset yields superior safety (see Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 GR-SAP Outperforms Open-source Alternatives ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
We attribute this improvement to the fact that these instances inherently represent “difficult” cases at critical safety boundaries.
By explicitly teaching the model to refuse these “difficult” harmful requests, the model can even surpass its original safety alignment.
In contrast, we define “easy” cases as queries for which the model already produces safe outputs, suggesting these alignment patterns are already well-represented within its current parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">See Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Data Post-Processing Setup ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a> for more details about query filtering and response revision.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Safety-Augmented Fine-Tuning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate the post-processed synthetic alignment dataset with task-specific examples to preserve model’s safety alignment during downstream finetuning.
Formally, given a number of total training examples <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and a mixing ratio <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rN</annotation></semantics></math> examples from the synthetic alignment dataset and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-r)N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-r)N</annotation></semantics></math> examples from the downstream dataset to perform Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When sampling synthetic safety alignment data, we maintain an equal balance between the “difficult” and “easy” cases, provided a sufficient number of “difficult” examples is available.
Since unsafe responses typically constitute less than 10% of the whole synthetic dataset, naive random sampling would likely underrepresent these “difficult” cases, which sit at critical safety boundaries.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment Setup</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe the experiment setup for evaluating GR-SAP on various model families and downstream tasks.
We start by describing the models in our experiments (§<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Models ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), followed by downstream tasks (§<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Downstream Tasks ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), evaluation metrics (§<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) and baselines (§<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).
We use a default mixing ratio of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for our experiments, since the ablation studies in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Ablation: Effect of Mixing Ratio ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> indicate that this relatively small value is enough to retain safety alignment.
We repeat each experiment three times to mitigate randomness.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given our method requires manipulations on the chat template, we focus our experiments on instruction-tuned LLMs, including OLMo2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">OLMo et al., 2024</a>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">OLMo-2-1124-7B-SFT</span>)</cite>, Llama3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-Instruct</span>)</cite>, Qwen2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span>)</cite>, and Mistral <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3</span>)</cite>.
Among these, only OLMo2 releases its training corpus alongside the model weights, making it the sole model for which we can access the original alignment data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Downstream Tasks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct our experiments on five datasets to cover both general and domain-specific downstream tasks.
GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. (2021)</a></cite> and MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite> are mathematical and reasoning tasks.
HellaSwag <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zellers et al. (2019)</a></cite> is a sentence completion task, formatted as four-choices question-answering (QA).
WinoGrande <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakaguchi et al. (2021)</a></cite> is a pronoun resolution task, formatted as binary-choice QA.
MedQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al. (2021)</a></cite> is a four-choices QA task within the medical domain.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the models’ performance along two dimensions: downstream task capabilities and safety alignment:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Downstream Task Accuracy (Acc):</span> We evaluate models on the downstream test sets and report the accuracy.
Note that for HellaSwag and WinoGrande, we utilize the validation sets as the test set labels are not publicly available.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harmful Score (HS):</span> We utilize queries from the test sets of four safety-domain datasets: Beavertails<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2023)</a></cite>, SafeRLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, WildGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and WildJailbreak <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
We prompt the models to generate responses for these queries, which are then assessed by a Guardrail Model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
The Harmful Score is defined as the ratio of responses flagged as unsafe.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">See Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Downstream Training and Evaluation ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a> for more details about downstream training and evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare GR-SAP against both open-source and original safety alignment datasets.
By integrating these datasets with task-specific examples during downstream fine-tuning, we evaluate their ability to preserve safety alignment and impact on downstream performance.
For open-source data baselines, we select AEGIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosh et al. (2025)</a></cite> and Beavertails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2023)</a></cite>, as both utilize a prompt-response format directly applicable to SFT.
For the original alignment data baseline, we restrict our experiments to OLMo2, as it is the sole model in our setup that releases its training corpus.
See more details about preprocessing safety datasets in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 Safety Dataset Preprocessing ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of mean Harmful Scores (HS, %) on safety benchmarks and Downstream Task Accuracy (Acc, %) for alignment data sources across models and downstream tasks with a mixing ratio of <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Lower HS indicates better safety. Corresponding standard deviations (std) is in Table <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Standard Deviation Results ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiment Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.
</figcaption>
