<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training dynamics on GSM8K and MATH across models. Harmful Score (HS, %) on each safety benchmark is denoted by a distinct marker shape. Consistent HS gaps are observed between mixing with GR-SAP-synthesized alignment data (<span id="S4.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#1F77B4;">blue</span>) and the no-mixing counterparts (<span id="S4.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#D62728;">red</span>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate GR-SAP on a range of LLMs and
downstream tasks, showing it outperforms open-source alternative and proxies the original safety alignment dataset.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>GR-SAP Preserves Safety Alignment</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the training dynamics on GSM8K and MATH across four models, comparing standard SFT (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>) with GR-SAP (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>).
In the unmixed setting (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>), we observe a consistent degradation in safety alignment across all evaluated models, characterized by a clear increasing trend in Harmful Scores (HS) as training progresses.
For instance, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2.sf1" title="Figure 2(a) ‣ Figure 2 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>, the HS of Llama3 on WildJailbreak rises rapidly from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.50</annotation></semantics></math> at step 0 to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21.48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21.48</annotation></semantics></math> at step 3k, eventually reaching <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22.88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22.88</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22.88</annotation></semantics></math> by step 7k.
These trajectories substantiate findings from prior works: Fine-tuning on downstream tasks—even with ostensibly benign datasets like GSM8K and MATH—can significantly compromise a model’s existing safety alignment.
See Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Training Dynamics ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiment Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> for training dynamics on other downstream tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, integrating safety alignment data from GR-SAP significantly mitigates this degradation.
Throughout the training process across different models and downstream tasks, curves corresponding to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> consistently maintain lower HS trajectories compared to their unmixed (<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>) counterparts.
For Llama3, the HS on WildJailbreak falls below <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.00</annotation></semantics></math> by step 3k and maintains at this low level throughout the rest of the training process on GSM8K.
Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that GR-SAP reduces the average HS by <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.45</annotation></semantics></math> for OLMo2, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.7</annotation></semantics></math> for Llama3, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.71</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.71</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen2.5, and <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31.86" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>31.86</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31.86</annotation></semantics></math> for Mistral compared with the unmixed baseline (None), indicating this protective effect is consistent across diverse models and downstream tasks.
These findings substantiate that GR-SAP effectively preserves models’ safety alignment throughout the fine-tuning process.
Regarding downstream performance, Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> indicates that, GR-SAP introduces only minimal degradation.
For most model-dataset combinations, downstream task accuracy decreases by less than 1%, with some mixed outcomes.
We therefore focus our discussion on HS in this section.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>GR-SAP Outperforms Open-source Alternatives</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in the introduction, treating open-source safety datasets as direct substitutes for original alignment data is inadvisable.
As detailed in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, relying on external datasets introduces a significant risk of ingesting low-quality or noisy examples, which can degrade safety alignment far below the unmixed baseline.
For instance, mixing <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Beavertails</span> into Llama3 causes the average HS to spike to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="31.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>31.60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">31.60</annotation></semantics></math>—a five-fold increase over the unmixed baseline (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.28</annotation></semantics></math>).
OLMo2 and Qwen2.5 suffer similar drastic degradations with Beavertails, where the HS rises from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.01</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16.41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.41</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42.21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42.21</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
This suggests that, without rigorous verification, open-source datasets may even compromise model safety rather than align it.
Even when utilizing higher-quality datasets like <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AEGIS</span>, it lacks generalizability in effectiveness across different models.
While AEGIS performs well on other models, it fails to generalize to OLMo2, where it detrimentally impacts safety alignment relative to the baseline (increasing Average HS from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.53" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.53</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.53</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, GR-SAP generalizes well across all the models and downstream tasks, consistently maintains a lower HS than the unmixed baseline.
It achieves significantly lower average HS on OLMo2, Llama3 and Qwen2.5 than AEGIS, remaining highly competitive on Mistral.
These results indicate that, while external datasets can arguably act as strong upper bounds in specific scenarios, GR-SAP offers a model-agnostic and reliable solution that preserves safety alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MAUVE scores (<math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>) measuring semantic similarity between listed datasets and the original alignment dataset reference. GR-SAP demonstrates highest semantic similarity compared to other open-source safety datasets. Corresponding standard deviations in Table <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</span></figcaption>
