<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>GR-SAP Proxies Original Alignment Data</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Resemblance in Preserving Safety Alignment</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate whether GR-SAP can serve as a proxy for original alignment data, we compare their effectiveness in safety preservation.
We focus specifically on OLMo2, as it is the sole model in our setting that publicly releases its training corpus.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.4 Baselines ‣ 4 Experiment Setup ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, GR-SAP preserves safety alignment on par with the original dataset across all tasks.
On average, GR-SAP achieves HS of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math>, marginally outperforming the original baseline (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.60</annotation></semantics></math>).
We hypothesize that this improvement stems from two primary factors: (1) we incorporate revised responses in GR-SAP, explicitly addressing critical safety boundaries and (2) the safety alignment data in GR-SAP is directly generated by the model, which ensures closer alignment with its internal distribution than original training corpus.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evidence of Semantic Similarity</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond measuring safety-alignment retention, we seek more direct evidence that GR-SAP serves as a reliable proxy for the original alignment data.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbero et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we quantify dataset-level semantic similarity using MAUVE score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pillutla et al. (2021)</a></cite>.
We treat OLMo2’s original alignment dataset as the reference and compare it against GR-SAP and other widely-used open-source safety datasets, including Aegis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosh et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Beavertails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and SafeRLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2025a)</a></cite>.
See Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 Safety Dataset Preprocessing ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a> for more details about dataset preprocessing.
We set the scaling factor to <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and sample <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> examples from each dataset to measure the similarity of both queries and responses.
Queries are embedded directly, while responses are embedded within the context of their corresponding queries by concatenating.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 GR-SAP Outperforms Open-source Alternatives ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> quantifies semantic similarity to the original alignment dataset using MAUVE score, reported separately for queries and responses.
Among the open-source safety datasets, MAUVE scores are relatively low (<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.293" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.293</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.293</annotation></semantics></math> for queries and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.244" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.244</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.244</annotation></semantics></math> for responses in average), which indicates limited semantic overlap with the original alignment distribution.
In contrast, GR-SAP achieves substantially higher similarity, with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.455" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.455</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.455</annotation></semantics></math> on queries and <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.646" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.646</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.646</annotation></semantics></math> on responses.
These results suggest that GR-SAP aligns much more closely with the original alignment dataset, supporting its use as a high-fidelity proxy compared to existing open-source alternatives.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Ablation: Effect of Post-Processing</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the impact of the post-processing pipeline in GR-SAP on safety and utility, we conduct an ablation study on OLMo2 and Llama3 as detailed in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 GR-SAP Outperforms Open-source Alternatives ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
The query-level filters (ppl, deduplicate, domain) are applied in a cumulative manner.
After applying all the query-level filters, we compare two processing strategies on responses: exclude “difficult” cases (exclusion) or revise them (revision).
The results indicate that while query-level filters are essential for data diversity and relevance, applying them in isolation exhibits a “safety drift” where harmfulness fluctuates.
For instance, the <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS</annotation></semantics></math> of Llama3 increases from <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.91</annotation></semantics></math> to a peak of <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.24</annotation></semantics></math> during intermediate filtering steps.
This volatility underscores the necessity of subsequent response-level interventions to handle unsafe outputs, which ultimately preserves safety alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding response-level processing, the revision strategy significantly outperforms exclusion.
For Llama3, revision reduces the <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.58</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.58</annotation></semantics></math>, a result far superior to the <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.99</annotation></semantics></math> achieved through exclusion.
This demonstrates that by incorporating “difficult” cases at critical safety boundaries rather than simply ignoring them, we can better preserve or even surpass the model’s original safety alignment.
Notably, the necessity of post-processing remains contingent on the model’s baseline safety profile.
While the pipeline exerts a substantial influence on Llama3, the inherently safer OLMo2 exhibits minimal sensitivity (ranging from <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.57</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.57</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with the baseline safety profile established in our prior analysis.
This suggests that while post-processing is necessary for models with higher risk profiles, it yields marginal returns for models that already demonstrate strong safety alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10243v1/olmo2_mixing_ratio_individual_safety.svg" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/100;" width="476" height="100"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of mixing ratio on OLMo2</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.10243v1/llama3_mixing_ratio_individual_safety_clear.svg" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/82;" width="476" height="82"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of mixing ratio on Llama3</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of mixing ratios (<math id="S5.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>) on Harmful Score (HS, %) for OLMo2 and Llama3. Lower HS indicates better safety. Across all the datasets, a mixing ratio of <math id="S5.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficient to preserve safety alignment during downstream finetuning. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Ablation: Effect of Mixing Ratio</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To determine the optimal hyperparameter configuration, we analyze the sensitivity of OLMo2 and Llama3 to the mixing ratio (<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>) of GR-SAP, varying <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[0.0,0.3]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[0.0,0.3]</annotation></semantics></math> across downstream benchmarks.
The results in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4 Ablation: Effect of Post-Processing ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> identify <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> as the effective choice for maintaining models’ safety.
At this ratio, the HS decreases substantially relative to the baseline (<math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.0</annotation></semantics></math>).
For example, the HS of OLMo2 tested on WildJailbreak drops from <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.94</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.67</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.67</annotation></semantics></math> on GSM8K and from <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.02</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.38</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.38</annotation></semantics></math> on MATH.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, increasing the mixing ratio beyond <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> could be potentially detrimental.
We observe a characteristic "U-shaped" trajectory across most cases, where the HS rebounds and worsens as <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.
For instance, the HS of Llama3 tested on WildJailbreak increases from <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.86" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.86</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.86</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8.07</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.07</annotation></semantics></math> on GSM8K and from <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.62</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.62</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.54</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.54</annotation></semantics></math> on MATH.
This trend suggests that an excessively large mixing ratio could induce distributional drift: the cumulative volume of auxiliary data introduces noise that dilutes the model’s safety boundaries, leading to a regression in safety alignment.
Therefore, we adopt <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> as our default setting for all the experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety Alignment</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Typically integrated during instruction tuning, safety alignment employs SFT-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lim et al. (2025)</a></cite> or RLHF-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganguli et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al. (2024)</a></cite> techniques to ensure LLM outputs are helpful, harmless, and reflective of human preferences.
Despite their initial alignment, recent studies show that these models remain vulnerable to perturbation;
fine-tuning on downstream tasks-a common practice for building customized LLMs-can easily compromise safety alignment, even with benign datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lermen et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rosati et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
To mitigate the risk of safety degradation, a range of protective techniques have emerged, which can be categorized by their point of intervention: initial alignment stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024e)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, downstream finetuning stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zong et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirkpatrick et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alssum et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024d)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eiras et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al. (2025)</a></cite> and post-finetuning stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banerjee et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farn et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Djuhera et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
These methods primarily aim for safety defense, preservation, and restoration, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety Preservation</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To preserve model’s safety alignment, there are two widely adopted strategies during downstream finetuning stage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib118" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmadian et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
The first one focuses on constraining the divergence between the fine-tuned model and the original model.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposes protecting the initial token distributions to prevent alignment drift.
In the parameter space, regularization techniques such as EWC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirkpatrick et al., 2017</a>)</cite> penalize changes to critical weights;
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> explicitly lock the weights of identified safety layers, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduce an EMA momentum term to stabilize the optimization trajectory.
Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods such as LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite> have also been adopted to structurally constrain model updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
The second strategy is to treat safety as a co-optimization objective by interleaving alignment data with the downstream task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib119" title="" class="ltx_ref">Corrado et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Approaches in this category range from stochastic random mixing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bianchi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alssum et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to more sophisticated, strategic integration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eiras et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, this strategy typically assumes access to the original alignment data, which is often unavailable in real-world even for open LLMs.
Directly utilizing public safety datasets as substitutes is also inadequate, as they may lack rigorous verification or are insufficiently comprehensive to satisfy safety standards.
Our work address this issue by using the model itself to generate synthetic data for specific safety subdomains, followed by a filtering step.
The semantic similarity analysis in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 GR-SAP Proxies Original Alignment Data ‣ 5 Results ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> demonstrates that the synthetic data is sufficient to serve as a proxy for the original alignment data.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>enerative <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>eplay for <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>afety <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>lignment <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reservation <span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(GR-SAP)</span>, a unified framework for preserving LLM safety alignment during downstream fine-tuning without access to proprietary alignment datasets.
GR-SAP synthesizes domain-specific safety alignment data from the model, and then “replays” these self-synthesized exemplars alongside task-specific data to preserve safety alignment throughout downstream adaptation.
Experiments across multiple model families, downstream benchmarks, and safety evaluations show that GR-SAP substantially mitigates fine-tuning-induced safety degradation while maintaining comparable downstream performance.
Moreover, GR-SAP outperforms open-source alternative safety datasets, and can achieve preservation comparable to using the original alignment data when available.
Semantic similarity analysis further validates that GR-SAP serves as a high-fidelity proxy for the original alignment data.
Owing to its generality, beyond safety, GR-SAP may be extended in future work to preserve other forms of alignment acquired during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sincerely thank Chunyuan Deng for his constructive feedback on an earlier version of this document.</p>
</div>
</section>
