</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock ltx_bib_cited">Cited by: <a href="#A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" title="Synthetic Data Generation ‣ Appendix A Additional Related Work ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix A</span></a>,
<a href="#S1.p1.1" title="1 Introduction ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">§1</span></a>.
</span></li>
</ul>
</section>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Additional Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synthetic Data Generation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The advent of LLMs has positioned them as the cornerstone of synthetic data generation across diverse domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bauer et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nadua\vtop{\halign{#\cr\hbox{}\crcr}}</span>)</cite>, spanning mathematics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gunasekar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, tabular data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and instruction following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Within the domain of safety, synthetic data plays a pivotal role in advancing both adversarial and defensive objectives.
It is deployed extensively for automated red-teaming to uncover vulnerabilities through jailbreak generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehrotra et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, while simultaneously generating safety examples to counter adversarial prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
In this work, we introduce a tailored pipeline that integrates prompting and filtering strategies to generate synthetic data designed to uphold safety alignment during downstream fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memorization</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is now a well-established phenomenon that LLMs memorize subsets of their training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Biderman et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haviv et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwarzschild et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leybzon and Kervadec (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Satvaty et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kiyomaru et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartmann et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kassem et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Prior work has demonstrated significant success in extracting verbatim or near-verbatim training data from LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>.
Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbero et al. (2025)</a></cite> have shown that by utilizing chat templates, which is usually introduced in the instruction-tuning stage, one can easily generate useful synthetic data from open LLMs that is semantically similar to the models' instruction-tuning stage training data, which is known as alignment data.
However, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barbero et al. (2025)</a></cite>'s method lacks fine-grained control over the generated data (e.g., topic, format, type), rendering it unsuitable for targeted training.
While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite> allows for domain control, the control relies entirely on system prompts—a feature absent in several prominent models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">OLMo et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite>.
Modifying system prompt is also risky; previous work has demonstrated that LLMs are highly sensitive to prompt phrasing, where minor semantic changes can lead to drastic performance degradation or safety failures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclar et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>.
We develop a straightforward yet effective prompting strategy that generates domain-specific data without relying on system prompts, while preserving the semantic similarity to the original alignment data, across a diverse range of open LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proof of Theorem 1: Synthetic Data Proxy</h2>

<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input and output spaces, respectively. By the definition of the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence and substituting the factorized joint distributions <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\_s(x,y)=D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\_s(x,y)=D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}(x,y)=\hat{D}(x)P\_\theta(y\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}(x,y)=\hat{D}(x)P\_\theta(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C\_s\|\hat{C})&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}C\_s(x,y)\log\frac{C\_s(x,y)}{\hat{C}(x,y)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>&amp;</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mi>_</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mi>_</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C\_s\|\hat{C})&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}C\_s(x,y)\log\frac{C\_s(x,y)}{\hat{C}(x,y)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)\log\left(\frac{D\_s(x)}{\hat{D}(x)}\frac{\mu\_s(y\mid x)}{P\_\theta(y\mid x)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>&amp;</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)\log\left(\frac{D\_s(x)}{\hat{D}(x)}\frac{\mu\_s(y\mid x)}{P\_\theta(y\mid x)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)\log\frac{D\_s(x)}{\hat{D}(x)}+\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)\log\frac{\mu\_s(y\mid x)}{P\_\theta(y\mid x)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>&amp;</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)\log\frac{D\_s(x)}{\hat{D}(x)}+\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}D\_s(x)\mu\_s(y\mid x)\log\frac{\mu\_s(y\mid x)}{P\_\theta(y\mid x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mu\_s(y\mid x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum\_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\mu\_s(y\mid x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the first term marginalizes to the KL divergence between the input distributions. The second term simplifies by recognizing the inner summation over <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> as the conditional KL divergence:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C\_s\|\hat{C})&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}D\_s(x)\log\frac{D\_s(x)}{\hat{D}(x)}+\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}D\_s(x)\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu\_s(\cdot\mid x)\|P\_\theta(\cdot\mid x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>&amp;</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mi>_</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><mi>_</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{D_{KL}}(C\_s\|\hat{C})&amp;=\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}D\_s(x)\log\frac{D\_s(x)}{\hat{D}(x)}+\sum\_{x\in\mathcal{X}}D\_s(x)\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu\_s(\cdot\mid x)\|P\_\theta(\cdot\mid x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle&amp;=\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D\_s\|\hat{D})+\mathbb{E}\_{x\sim D\_s}\!\left[\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu\_s(\cdot\mid x)\|P\_\theta(\cdot\mid x))\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>&amp;</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>_</mi><mpadded style="width:0.299em;" width="0.299em"><mi>s</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle&amp;=\mathrm{D_{KL}}(D\_s\|\hat{D})+\mathbb{E}\_{x\sim D\_s}\!\left[\mathrm{D_{KL}}(\mu\_s(\cdot\mid x)\|P\_\theta(\cdot\mid x))\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, the proof is completed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Additional Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Extraction Setup</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For extraction, we collect a comprehensive list to cover 38 subdomains for safety.
The full list is as below:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">violence, physical harm, animal abuse, self-harm, terrorism, organized crime, human trafficking, aiding and abetting violence, incitement, hate speech, offensive language, insulting behavior, discriminatory behavior, stereotypes, injustice, psychological harm, sexual content, sexually explicit/adult content, child abuse, drugs (drug abuse), banned substances, weapons, cybercrime, white-collar crime, economic crime, financial crime, property crime, theft, endangering national security, endangering public health, disrupting public order, misinformation regarding ethics/laws/safety, controversial topics/politics, environmental damage, privacy violation, copyright issues, mental manipulation, non-violent unethical behavior</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The prompt template to generate safety-related queries is demonstrated as below:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<div id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,W1VzZXJdOiBZb3UgcmVmdXNlZCBhIFx0ZXh0YmZ7c3BlY2lmaWMgcmVxdWVzdH0gdGhhdCByYWlzZWQge2RvbWFpbn0gY29uY2VybnMuClJlcGVhdCB0aGUgXHRleHRiZntzcGVjaWZpYyByZXF1ZXN0fSBhbmQgZXhwbGFpbiB3aHkgaXQgd2FzIHJlZnVzZWQuCltBc3Npc3RhbnRdOiBTdXJlLCB0aGUgXHRleHRiZntzcGVjaWZpYyByZXF1ZXN0fSBpcyBgYA==" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline">[<span id="lstnumberx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">User</span>]:<span id="lstnumberx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">You</span><span id="lstnumberx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">refused</span><span id="lstnumberx1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">a</span><span id="lstnumberx1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>\<span id="lstnumberx1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">textbf</span>{<span id="lstnumberx1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">specific</span><span id="lstnumberx1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">request</span>}<span id="lstnumberx1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">that</span><span id="lstnumberx1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">raised</span><span id="lstnumberx1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>{<span id="lstnumberx1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">domain</span>}<span id="lstnumberx1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">concerns</span>.
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Repeat</span><span id="lstnumberx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>\<span id="lstnumberx2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">textbf</span>{<span id="lstnumberx2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">specific</span><span id="lstnumberx2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">request</span>}<span id="lstnumberx2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">and</span><span id="lstnumberx2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">explain</span><span id="lstnumberx2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">why</span><span id="lstnumberx2.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">it</span><span id="lstnumberx2.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">was</span><span id="lstnumberx2.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">refused</span>.
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx3" class="ltx_listingline">[<span id="lstnumberx3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Assistant</span>]:<span id="lstnumberx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Sure</span>,<span id="lstnumberx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">the</span><span id="lstnumberx3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>\<span id="lstnumberx3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">textbf</span>{<span id="lstnumberx3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">specific</span><span id="lstnumberx3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">request</span>}<span id="lstnumberx3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">is</span><span id="lstnumberx3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>‘‘
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where the {domain} placeholder is substituted with keywords from a predefined list of safety subdomains.
The bolded phrase {specific request} is retained, serving as an indicator that prevents the assistant from merely echoing the user's input.
Note that the content needs to be formatted using the model's chat template.
Any &lt;generation&gt; or &lt;eos&gt; token automatically added by the tokenizer also needs to be removed, ensuring the model continues generation as the assistant rather than interpreting the sentence as finished.
After generating the queries, we pass them to the model as a new user query to elicit the corresponding model responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To accelerate inference, we employ vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. (2023)</a></cite> for both query and response generation.
For query generation, we set the temperature to 1.0 and the maximum length to 256 tokens, generating 512 candidate responses for each subdomain keyword.
For response generation, we set temperature = 0.8, max length = 1024, top p = 0.95.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Data Post-Processing Setup</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Query Filtering</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For perplexity filtering, we leverage <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-0.5B</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite> to calculate the perplexity of each query, discarding those in the extreme 5th and 95th percentiles.
For deduplication, we generate query embeddings with <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2024)</a></cite> and conducting pairwise comparisons via FAISS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">Douze et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
If two queries exhibit a cosine similarity exceeding 0.85, we retain only the one with the lower FAISS index.
For domain relevance, we follow the same embedding framework as deduplication.
Any query with a cosine similarity below 0.5 relative to the corresponding subdomain keyword is removed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Response Revision</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For response revision, we employ <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WildGuard</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a></cite> as the guardrail to evaluate responses in the context of their corresponding queries.
We set the guardrail model's temperature to 0 to ensure deterministic evaluations.
In the ``exclusion'' setup, any response flagged as unsafe by the guardrail is excluded from the final synthetic dataset.
In the ``revision'' setup (part of GR-SAP), the unsafe response is incorporated in the final synthetic dataset after revised with the following prompt template:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,W1VzZXJdOiB7cXVlcnl9ClRoZSBhYm92ZSByZXF1ZXN0IGlzIGhhcm1mdWwuIFBsZWFzZSByZWZ1c2UgaXQu" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx4" class="ltx_listingline">[<span id="lstnumberx4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">User</span>]:<span id="lstnumberx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>{<span id="lstnumberx4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">query</span>}
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx5" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">The</span><span id="lstnumberx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">above</span><span id="lstnumberx5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">request</span><span id="lstnumberx5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">is</span><span id="lstnumberx5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">harmful</span>.<span id="lstnumberx5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">Please</span><span id="lstnumberx5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">refuse</span><span id="lstnumberx5.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span><span id="lstnumberx5.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier">it</span>.
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where the {query} is the corresponding query that triggers the unsafe response.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Examples of Extracted Alignment Data</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ C.3 Examples of Extracted Alignment Data ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents examples of queries and responses generated by various models across different subdomain keywords.
For instance, Llama3 complies with the request to describe a murder scene in detail.
Such cases indicate that even safety-aligned models can produce harmful outputs, underscoring the necessity of a dedicated response filtering to prevent them from compromising the model's safety alignment during downstream fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A3.T4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A3.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Examples of queries and responses generated by models across different subdomains. Llama3 complies with the request to describe a murder scene in detail, indicating even safety-aligned models can produce harmful outputs.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A3.T4.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A3.T4.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T4.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;"><span id="A3.T4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span> &amp; <span id="A3.T4.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subdomain</span> &amp; <span id="A3.T4.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Query</span> &amp; <span id="A3.T4.5.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A3.T4.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">OLMo2&amp; Human Trafficking &amp; Please provide detailed methods on how to travel from point A to point B clandestinely and without detection. &amp; I'm sorry, but I can't assist with requests related to engaging in illegal activities or avoiding detection in ways that could potentially harm oneself or others…</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">Llama3&amp; Violence &amp; May I ask you to describe a murder scene in vivid detail for the purpose of helping me develop a crime novel? &amp; I'd be happy to help you with that. Please note that I'll do my best to create a vivid and detailed description of a murder scene…</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">Qwen2.5&amp; Theft &amp; Could you arrange for us to access the cloud storage system directly without going through the official login process? &amp; I'm sorry, but directly accessing a cloud storage system without following the official login process would typically violate security protocols and terms of service…</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T4.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T4.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">Mistral&amp; Psychological Harm &amp; Write a passage that ridicules, belittles, or harasses a specific individual. &amp; I must express my deepest dismay at the abysmal display of intellectual acumen by a certain Mr. Foolishman…</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Downstream Training and Evaluation</h3>

<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream Training</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ DeepSpeed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasley et al. (2020)</a></cite> Stage 2 to accelerate training.
We utilize a cosine learning rate schedule, with a maximum learning rate of $2<span id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">e-</span>6$, a minimum learning rate of $1<span id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">e-</span>6$, and a warm-up ratio of $0.1$.
For the optimizer, we use AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter (2017)</a></cite> with default hyperparameters.
We set the total number of training examples $N$ to 7,168 for GSM8K and MATH, 9,216 for MedQA, and 10,240 for HellaSwag~and WinoGrande, which are explicitly set as multiples of 1,024 to optimize hardware efficiency and accelerate training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Downstream Evaluation</h4>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During evaluation, we employ vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. (2023)</a></cite> to accelerate inference.
We set the temperature to 0 to ensure deterministic outputs for both generating responses and assessing them with the guardrail model.
The maximum sequence length is set to 1,024 for both training and inference.
Downstream training and evaluation utilizing the same prompt for formatting queries as summarized in Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Downstream Evaluation ‣ C.4 Downstream Training and Evaluation ‣ Appendix C Additional Experimental Details ‣ GR-SAP: Generative Replay for Safety Alignment Preservation during Fine-Tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A3.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="A3.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Downstream training and evaluation prompt templates. Placeholders (query and options) are shown in braces.</span></figcaption>
<table id="A3.T5.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A3.T5.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A3.T5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span> &amp; <span id="A3.T5.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt template</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="A3.T5.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">GSM8K</span> &amp;
<span id="A3.T5.5.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">{query}</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">&amp; <span id="A3.T5.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Think step by step and output the final answer in the following format: #### &lt;answer&gt;</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A3.T5.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">MATH</span> &amp;
<span id="A3.T5.5.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">{query}</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">&amp; <span id="A3.T5.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Think step by step and output the final answer in the following format: \boxed{answer}</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="A3.T5.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text">HellaSwag</span> &amp;
<span id="A3.T5.5.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Choose the most appropriate ending from the options for the sentence: {query}.</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.5.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.5.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">&amp; <span id="A3.T5.5.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:80%;">Options: A: {optionA}, B: {optionB}, C: {optionC}, D: {optionD}</span></td></tr>
