</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Safety Dataset Preprocessing</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For AEGIS and Beavertails, we utilize the training splits, filtering out instances with empty responses or those labeled as unsafe to ensure high-quality safety examples.
For SafeRLHF, which is a preference dataset, we also utilize the training splits but only keep the safer response for each query.
Regarding the original alignment dataset,
We identify three sub-datasets explicitly focusing on safety from the <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/allenai/tulu-3-sft-olmo-2-mixture" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">instruction dataset</a> of OLMo2:
CoCoNot~<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brahman et al. (2024)</a></cite>, WildGuard~<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and WildJailbreak~<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
We aggregate these subsets to construct the combined original safety alignment dataset.
When computing MAUVE, we use the raw data (without post-processing) of GR-SAP to avoid introducing artifacts that could artificially alter the semantic similarity between the two distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
